KNOWNBUG: std::string fill-construction std::string(n, c) is a no-op.

The fill constructor dispatches to basic_string<char>::_M_construct(
size_type, char), which for the explicitly-instantiated basic_string<char>
(extern template) is defined OUT OF LINE (in bits/basic_string.tcc) and is
realised as a declaration with a nil in-class body.

queue_deferred_methods_of_instance() (which fixed literal/range
construction -- see cpp11_string_literal_char_access) deliberately handles
only INLINE members.  Out-of-line member bodies are kept in a primary
template's template_methods and would have to be fetched and substituted;
matching them by base name alone is overload-ambiguous and converting them
eagerly destabilises unrelated standard-library classes (std::unordered_map,
std::regex), so the fill _M_construct body is not instantiated yet.  Both
size() and the characters therefore remain wrong.

Fixing this requires signature-aware out-of-line member instantiation; see
doc/architectural/cpp-extern-template-member-instantiation.md.  When that
lands, both assertions become SUCCESS and verification succeeds; reclassify
to CORE.

